# Custom components

The built-in [components](/docs/ui/components) are a starting point. Sooner
or later you will want a button with different padding, or a component the
set does not have at all. Howdy UI gives you two ways to get there, and
both end with ordinary Gleam files in your own project that you are free
to change.

## Copying a built-in component

Howdy UI does not have options for every detail of every component. When
one is not quite right, copy its source into your project and edit it. The
package comes with a command line for this:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/ui list
```

```
button        Buttons, and the theme toggle built on them.
heading       Headings.
typography    Paragraphs, secondary text and links.
input         Form fields.
layout        Containers, cards, stacks and rows.
```

Copy one or more by name:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/ui add button layout
```

That writes `src/<your app>/ui/button.gleam` and `layout.gleam`. They are
the same source the package ships, with a header noting the version they
came from. The files are yours. Import your copy where you would have used
the built-in:

```gleam
import my_app/ui/button.{Primary}

button.button(Primary, [], [text("Save")])
```

The copies import `lustre` and `sketch` themselves, so both must be direct
dependencies of your project. `add` tells you if one is missing:

```sh
gleam add sketch
```

Two options change what `add` does:

- `--to=<dir>` writes the copies somewhere other than `src/<app>/ui`.
- `--force` overwrites a copy you have edited. Without it, `add` leaves an
  edited copy alone and shows you how it differs.

### Keeping up with the package

A copy does not change when you upgrade Howdy UI, which is the point: an
upgrade cannot alter how your site looks. To see what you are missing, or
just what you have changed, compare a copy with the version the package
ships:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/ui diff button
```

The output is a diff in the usual style. Lines starting `-` are only in
your copy, and lines starting `+` are only in the package's version. Bring
across by hand whatever you want from it.

## Writing a component from scratch

A component is a function that returns an element with a class on it. The
class is written with [Sketch](https://hexdocs.pm/sketch), which is CSS as
Gleam functions:

```gleam
// src/my_app/ui/badge.gleam
import howdy/ui/style.{class}
import howdy/ui/theme/tokens
import lustre/element.{type Element, text}
import lustre/element/html
import sketch/css.{type Class}

pub fn badge(content: String) -> Element(msg) {
  html.span([class(badge_class())], [text(content)])
}

pub fn classes() -> List(Class) {
  [badge_class()]
}

pub fn badge_class() -> Class {
  css.class([
    css.background(tokens.primary),
    css.color(tokens.on_primary),
    css.padding_(tokens.space_1 <> " " <> tokens.space_2),
    css.property("border-radius", tokens.radius_small),
    css.font_size_("0.75rem"),
    css.font_weight("600"),
  ])
}
```

There are three parts, and the built-in components are made the same way:

- `badge_class` describes the styles. `css.class` takes a list of
  declarations. Most CSS properties have a function, and `css.property`
  covers any that do not.
- `badge` builds the element. `class` from `howdy/ui/style` attaches the
  class and registers its CSS, so that pages and live views include it.
  `ui.class` is the same function, if you already import `howdy/ui`.
- `classes` lists every class the module uses. It is what lets the styles
  be written to a file for [publishing](/docs/ui/publishing).

Use it like any other component:

```gleam
ui.row([], [ui.h2("Orders"), badge.badge("New")])
```

### Use tokens, never colours

The badge never says what colour it is. It uses *tokens* from
`howdy/ui/theme/tokens`, which stand for whatever the current
[theme](/docs/ui/themes) says:

- Colours: `background`, `surface`, `border`, `text`, `text_muted`,
  `primary`, `primary_hover`, `on_primary`, `danger`, `on_danger`, `focus`.
- Fonts: `font_body`, `font_heading`, `font_mono`.
- Radii: `radius_small`, `radius_medium`, `radius_large`.
- Spacing: `space_1`, `space_2`, `space_3`, `space_4`, `space_6`,
  `space_8`, running from `0.25rem` to `2rem`.

A component built from tokens looks right under every theme, including ones
written after it. Write `css.color("#2563eb")` and the component is stuck
with that blue, in dark mode and in every theme a visitor picks.

Spacing tokens are a fixed scale, not part of the theme, so layouts stay
the same size when the theme changes. Using the scale, not arbitrary
numbers, is what keeps spacing looking even across a site.

### States and variants

Sketch has functions for the states of an element, each taking the
declarations that apply in that state:

```gleam
css.class([
  css.color(tokens.text_muted),
  css.hover([css.color(tokens.text)]),
  css.focus_visible([css.outline("2px solid " <> tokens.focus)]),
  css.disabled([css.property("opacity", "0.5")]),
])
```

For a component with variants, take a custom type and build the class from
it, as the built-in button does:

```gleam
pub type Tone {
  Neutral
  Alert
}

pub fn badge_class(tone: Tone) -> Class {
  let #(background, color) = case tone {
    Neutral -> #(tokens.surface, tokens.text)
    Alert -> #(tokens.danger, tokens.on_danger)
  }
  css.class([css.background(background), css.color(color)])
}

pub fn classes() -> List(Class) {
  [badge_class(Neutral), badge_class(Alert)]
}
```

List every variant in `classes`. A variant left out still works while you
develop, because its CSS is made when it is first used, but it would be
missing from the published file.

## The all.gleam module

Whenever `add` runs, it also writes `all.gleam` in the same directory. It
gathers the classes of every module there that has a `classes` function:

```gleam
pub fn classes() -> List(Class) {
  list.flatten([
    badge.classes(),
    button.classes(),
  ])
}
```

Your own components are included, as long as they live in that directory
and have a `classes` function. Do not edit the file, since the next `add`
replaces it. [Publishing](/docs/ui/publishing) uses this list.

`add` needs the name of at least one component, so to bring `all.gleam` up
to date after writing a component of your own, run it again for one you
have already copied. An edited copy is left as it is:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/ui add button
```

## How class names work

Sketch names each class after a hash of its contents, such as
`css-7B0E1023`. Two classes with the same declarations share a name, and
changing a declaration changes the name. You never write these names, and
should not rely on them in tests or in hand-written CSS. To style something
from your own stylesheet, add a class of your own with `attribute.class`
alongside the generated one.
